   446	### 3.7 THEOREM NO-CEILING — why this package can only give floors
   447	
   448	> **THEOREM NO-CEILING.** Let `L_r = <H, E_1..E_r>` with `H^2 = 1`,
   449	> `E_i·E_j = -delta_ij`, `H·E_i = 0`. The constraints that the boundary ledger
   450	> imposes on `Z` — `Z` nef, `Z^2 = N > 0`, `Z·C >= 0` for the boundary classes,
   451	> `Z = D H - sum a_i E_i` with `a_i >= 1` and the proximity inequalities — do not
   452	> bound `D = Z·H` at fixed `N`.
   453	>
   454	> *Proof.* Hodge index on a lattice of signature `(1,r)` gives, for `Z, H` in the
   455	> positive cone, `(Z·H)^2 >= Z^2 H^2 = N` — a **lower** bound `D >= sqrt(N)`, and
   456	> that is the only inequality available in this direction. For unboundedness:
   457	> `Z = D H - sum_{i=1}^{r} y_i E_i` has `Z^2 = D^2 - sum y_i^2`, and for every
   458	> `D` with `D^2 >= N` the value `D^2 - N` is a sum of four squares, so `r = 4`
   459	> already realises `Z^2 = N` with `Z·H = D` arbitrary. Every constraint in
   460	> `(I1)-(I6)`, `(D1)`, `DEG-SPLIT` is of the form `Z·(effective) >= 0` or an
   461	> equality between such pairings; none is a negativity. ∎
